> 
> Mind telling me how the cost of finding and removing person A's posts is
> justified in light of the small cost of those resources used when compared 
> with the overall resource base? 

Although people costs *should* be considered more than machine costs,
faced with a six-figure upgrade to machines that's not in the budget vs.
the cost of a sysadmin's time that *is* in the budget, which do you think
most sites will opt for? 
 
> >Your right to free speech also carries with it a risk: the risk of being 
> >ignored.  The size of your audience is your problem.
> 
> Ignored yes, but removed from servers is another story.

Removed?  Or never picked up in the first place?  Are you telling me the 
default Usenet configuration is *all* groups and you have to eliminate 
them one by one?  And is that an ongoing effort, or one-time?  Or is it 
simply a matter of never picking them up in the first place?  I actually 
don't know how Usenet works from a sysadmin perspective.  Fill me in on 
just how much trouble these folks are going to in order to annoy you all 
so much.

Anyway.  If I own a site and choose not to carry any given group, I am not
censoring it.  Not by any definition of the term.  I am not restraining
anyone from posting to that group, nor from reading it.  I'm just silently
informing them that they'll have to use someone else's computer resources
to do so.  Maybe I disagree with some fundamental aspect of the group's
charter or maybe I just can't see my way clear to buy enough disk space. 
In point of fact, why I choose not to carry the newsgroup is none of your
business.  IT'S MY MACHINE AND I HAVE THE RIGHT TO USE IT HOW I SEE FIT. 
If I allow you to use the machine as my guest (and that's what I'd be
doing) you can use what I put on the machine or you can go elsewhere, but
you can keep your righteous moaning about what I *should* put on MY
machine to yourself.  Get your own machine and carry any damn newsgroups
you want on it.  More power to you. 

If this is about free speech, tell me how come this so-called "anti-
censorship" crowd is so intent on ramming speech that may be offensive to
some person (a site owner) down her throat because other people think she
"should" propagate it, hmmm?  If a local newspaper does not (and never
did) carry a columnist with whom you suspect they might disagree, are you
out there picketing?  Must every newspaper carry every single syndicated
columnist to satisfy the rigors of free speech? 

By the way, I strongly suspect that enthusiasm for this idea is inversely
proportional to (A) the amount one is actually paying out-of-pocket for
Internet access and (B) the level of sensitivity required by one's
vocation to a computer center's budget. 

--David

