In article <37ear3$m51@Jester.CC.MsState.Edu> fwp@CC.MsState.Edu (Frank Peters) writes:

>This thread is about academic systems, not personal or corporate
>systems.  Academic systems (especially at state universities) don't
>belong to the individuals managing them and there are legal and moral
>constraints placed upon the management of those systems because of
>their status as educational resources.

Remember, though, that there is more than one type of academic system.
Many posters assume that all of a university's computers are analagous
to public libraries. But, many universities have more than one system
and some systems are limited to certain uses or users.

At UMass/Boston, for example, there's a VAX/VMS cluster run by Computing
Services that is charged with providing services to the community. They
don't even make Usenet available to their users (don't ask me to speculate
why; we've been feeding it to them for almost two years.) My little cluster
is limited to users from the Math and Computer Science dept, and we're
massively overloaded.

[We stopped giving access to people outside the department because we were
drowning. Our budget is based on the number of students enrolled in cs
courses, who pay per-course fees to use these machines, and they are our #1
priority. We happen to be able to provide a full feed right now but
alt.binaries.* is going to go soon. Partly because of disk space and partly
because the downloading is tying up the few high-speed modems and partly
because the downloading is keeping the machine load high... I'd be happy to
accept, and install, a machine or disk space if donated; I can't do
anything about the modems.]

  Although I've fought free speech battles here, and so have my bosses, as
a department lab we're really not geared up to take too much heat. I'm
afraid that if one user were to make too big of a fuss, we could get
ordered by high-high-ups to restrict our users to just computer science
course work: no Usenet, no games, no Internet, nothing. We do ask our users
to think carefully before using us as a battleground. We have to enforce
minimal standards of Net behavior, and contractual obligations (like no
commercial use, and University policies) but we don't do any censorship
based on social or political content. Our resource-based limitations are
defined by time of day and disk space use, not content. Sometimes that's
the best you can do.

It drives me nuts, though, when people think that these services are
*free*. It's not free when 10% of our annual budget is going to new disk
space, when students can't do their homework because the modem pool is 100%
busy, or because the PC's are filled with game players, when people with
system work to do have to take the afternoon to deal with an avalanche of
e-mail or memos, when all email stops on the system because some user gets
mail-bombed, or decides, as one of my users once did, to send a large file
to every user on another school's VAX cluster. All this time, energy, and
resources *could* be going into our primary missions, which are: to educate
people (in math and computer science) and do research to increase knowledge
(in math and computer science.) There's a limit to how much we can take
away from the rest of our users so that ONE user can get a lesson in
political science or sociology or just plain common-sense manners.

Betsy

[these rantings are my own opinion, not department policy, unless a
particular item is identified as such.]
