You're right, they aren't. I don't think that's the real point. 

If you set up a stable, you expect people to put horses there. If you
set up a meeting for reformed alcoholics, you'd expect to talk about
alcoholism. 

Just as if you set up a worldwide electronic communications forum, you'd
expect electronic communications.

USENET *wants* to be free, not in cost, but in scope and range of use. Yes,
there's a lot of "jerks" out there, but if you let them ruin the "free"-ness
of USENET by implementing policies that restrict the "free"-ness, you give 
them the absolute power to dictate to the rest of USENET how things will go. 

If these jerks don't want anyone talking about pornography, they'll simply
post porn to every group on usenet.

If they want regulation in posting, they'll spam the net with advertisements.

If they want to get rid of a group, they'll "alt.syntax.tactical" it.

>(in math and computer science.) There's a limit to how much we can take
>away from the rest of our users so that ONE user can get a lesson in
>political science or sociology or just plain common-sense manners.

Meanwhile, the "free medium of exchange" becomes less and less so. Pretty
soon, anything less than PC speech found on USENET will be grounds for 
expulsion of a student or termination of an employee. 

I've continued (and will continue) to suggest tolerance of those people 
who must do "anti-social" things. This tolerance is accomplished by ignoring 
them and by encouraging the retiring of outdated newsreaders. 

That's all you can really do. People are people, and if you want an indication
of why restrictions do not work, look to your lawbooks and the gyrations
you have to go through to do much of anything these days. You cannot stop
a determined human being from doing anything, short of shooting them. 
The waste of energy you perceive is that energy expended in trying. 
