I have no doubt that early printers (you know, paper and wood-block type,
set the type by hand, then print each individual page by hand too) felt
that they too were going to create a global village through the enhanced
communication that they had created.  

Technology is nothing but technology -- the technology of the A-Bomb
is the same technology of lifesaving nucular medicine.  The micro-circuitry
of the PC was [in part] developed to guide ICBMs.  And remember where the
internet came from, and what it *used* to be called.....
It would be simply
unrealistic for someone to assume that a new technology will solve all the
problems of humanity -- history simply demonstrates the opposite.

However, there is another aspect to thiss -- the new technology has placed
a powerful communication tool in the hands of the ordinary citizen.  Much
as the right of a citizen to keep and bear firearms serves as a check against
tyrrany [see Thomas Jefferson on the need of an occasional revolution],
the ability of anyone to 'get the word out' serves as a check on the excesses
and incompetence of persons in positions of authority.

Free speech is a valuable thing, and it is so important to a free culture
that we must tolerate unpleasant things that are an incidental aspect to
it.  We must tolerate the Nazis marching through Stokie IL [see US Supreme
Court decision, circa 1979 or so] because to do otherwise would harm a 
freedom that the rest of us don't abuse, and thus we must tolerate flame
wars because to do otherwise would harm a freedom that responsible people
do not abuse.

A while back, on this campus, I got a mid-level administrator quite upset
when I published [ie posted on local newsgroup] the results of a school-
sponsored survey.  The information was public, but like many governmental
documents, was not widly distributed.  As I understand it, the concern was
not that the information was incorrect, nor that the information was not in
the public domain, but that I had posted it.  In other words, [and I was
never directly confronted] I believe that the concern was that I had taken
publicly availible information and used it to publicly contridict something
which the administrator had posted.

In other words, using truth to contridict statements made by one in a 
position of power.  This is the power of this new technology, and IMHO,
the reason why so many entrenched authorities are so frightened by it.
Now, I don't know all the specifics of the Santa Rosa case, but could
the issue be not what was posted as much as a concern by an entrenched
power block [feminists] that they are no longer able to silence those
who think thoughts that they oppose?

I am taking this as abstract political theory, and thus the merit of the
views, and even the moral character of the participants become irrelevant.
Regardless of the question of how correct they may be, the fact is that much
of academia is currently dominated by powerful feminist groups that have
the ability to restrict the expression of views with which they disagree
with.  Remember, I am *not* saying that the views *deserve* to be expressed,
this is abstract political theory.

Now, when you have people able to take an end run around the censorship
(and look up the Latin roots of that word, BTW) of a powerful group,
the group can not help but see the means as being a threat to their 
continued authority.  Thus, I really wonder if the issue here is what 
may have been posted, or the fact that it is a challenge to an entrenched
authority.

This is what the issue in the 1960s about long hair was about.  Not that
long hair was inherently evil, but that it was a challenge to the authority
of the then-established order.  Now, we are seeing the same thing in other
situations.

ED

