Arno Penzias, of Bell Labs, put it this way on a TV program I once watched:
"Computers help with speed, but not direction.  If you are lost driving in
your car, going faster just gets you lost in a larger area."  That may not
be an exact quote, but it is very much the sense of what he said, and it is
applicable to all forms of technology.

The single most important aspect of the Internet, and that which is most
dangerous to vested political interests, is that fact that people in widely
separated locations can communicate rapidly and inexpensively with one
another, exchanging very large volumes of information, all more or less
invisibly.  The so-called media, with the associated filters that they apply
to the news, become less important as moulders of public opinion.  One must be
careful, however; it will surprise no-one who reads this if I point out that
there is a good deal of unsubstantiated rumor, if not outright garbage, to be
found on the net; but this is part of democracy.  Intelligent people learn
to recognize the false and useless, and discard it.

All of the above is strictly my own opinion, unless otherwise noted.  No-one
else should be held responsible for what I think.

Neil Dickey


