Yes, that's the case.

Todays' NY Times has an Op-ed page column by Anthony Lewis on the same
subject, titled, Time to Grow Up. 

As I understand it the college had set up separate women and men only
bulletin boards, with password only access after the user's gender had
been verified by the moderators. After a falling out with his girl friend,
a male student posted crude comments about her and one of her female
friends. Since they had no access to the men's board, the women did not
see the postings. A male aquaintance of the two women saw the posting and
passed them a copy. The two women and the man (!) complained of sexual
harassment to the college and the Department of Education Civil Rights
Division. The college bought off (--oops, settled with) the three for
$15,000 each. The federal case against the college for allowing it to
happen pends. 

Lewis' column also discusses the University of New Hamphire case where one
of their faculty was accused of sexual harassment for using a simile
containing sexual language in a technical writing class. 

Lewis' column concludes: "Thirty years ago, in the case of New York Times
v. Sullivan, Justice William O. Brennan Jr. said Americans were committed
to the view that speech should be "uninhibited, robust and wide open." We
need to get back to that commitment. Genuine sexual harassment is an
outrage. But claims of insensitive words should not be allowed to override
the First Amendment, least of all in the free atmosphere of a university. 

"The menace of Politically Correct thinking has in fact begun to ebb, I
believe. But the censorious are always trying to find new ways of
suppression. 

"The episodes at Santa Rosa and the University of New Hampshire were
frivolous, a purile drain on the energy of people who had better things to
do. The students who brought the charges - and the rest of us - need a
simple piece of advice: Grow up." 

One difficulty that I have with trying to come to my own conclusions about
these types of situations is that everything I have seen is second or
third hand. I have no way of knowing the biases or inaccuracies of the
authors. I have seen several articles on these two incidents and most of
the facts are consistent. I still don't know if there is another side of
the story that would change my opinion. 


