 Time to Grow up   by Anthony Lewis, the New York Times   
October 14, 1994. 

 
TULSA, Okla. Since when are Americans such tender
plants? More and more of us seem to find our
sensibilities so of fended by someone's words that we
rush to the law to demand punish ment of the speaker.  


Worse yet, authorities, university administrators,
government officials often agree with the thin skinned
complainants. What ever happened to the American idea
of freedom for unpleasant speech? And to our sense of
the absurd?   There was the case last month of three
students at Santa Rosa Junior College in California who
were offended by sexual comments on a computer bulletin
board. They com plained to the U.S. Department of
Education. And they won.   

Santa Rosa had separate computer bulletin boards, for
men only and women only. Students took part
voluntarily, were given the necessary password and
promised to keep ev erything confidential.   

A male student who tuned in to the men's, Dylan
Humphrey, saw crude comments about two female students,
Jennifer Branham and Lois Arata. He told Ms. Branham.

"It was like it was on the bathroom wall," Mr. Humphrey
explained, "and I thought she had a right to know."
Uhhuh.   The three students brought charges under Title
IX of the Civil Rights Act, which prohibits sex
discrimination in schools that get Federal funds. The
Education Department's Office of Civil Rights ruled
that the separate bulletin boards were sex
discrimination. It also found that Ms. Branham had been
subjected to sexual harassment that created a hostile
educational environment for her at the college news
paper, where three men who posted the messages work
with her.   

The college settled the complaints by paying the three
students $15,000 each. It is still wrestling with the
Education Department.   

Reading about that case, my first reaction was how
preposterous it was that the Federal Government in
Washington should concern itself with such stuffand put
a college through the legal wringer over it. My second
was that those students should learn about free speech. 


 Justice Brandeis said long ago that, under our system
of freedom, the right way to deal with bad speech is to
answer it. The remedy is more speech, he said, not
silence.   Michael Godwin of the Electronic Frontier
Foundation, a public interest law firm, made the same
point if slightly less elegantly about the Santa Rosa
case. People who objected to the computer message, he
said, "should have called the guy who posted the
message a jerk."   

Call an obnoxious speaker a jerk: what a useful
formula, what an intelligent way to get the resentment
off your chest without starting down the legal road to
suppression of speech. It would have worked well, for
exam ple, in the notorious case of Prof. J. Donald
Silva of the University of New Hampshire.   

Professor Silva, teaching a class on technical writing,
was explaining what a simile was. He quoted the belly
dancer Little Egypt on her work. "Belly dancing," he
said, "is like Jello on a plate with a vibrator under
the plate."   

Seven women in the class complained. A tribunal led by
students found Professor Silva guilty of verbal sexual
harassment and ordered him to apologize and undergo
counseling. Instead he suedand was suspended from his
job. Last month a Federal court held that the univer
sity had violated the First Amend ment in punishing
him.  

Thirty years ago, in the case of New York Times v.
Sullivan, Justice William J. Brennan Jr. said Americans
were committed to the view that speech should be
"uninhibited, robust and wide open." We need to get
back to that commitment. Genu ine sexual harassment is
an outrage. But claims of insensitive words should not
be allowed to override the First Amendment, least of
all in the free atmosphere of a university.   

The menace of Politically Correct thinking has in fact
begun to ebb, I believe. But the censorious are always
trying to find new ways of suppression.   The episodes
at Santa Rosa and the University of New Hampshire were
frivolous, a puerile drain on the energy of people who
had better things to do. The students who brought the
charges and the rest of us need a simple piece of
advice: Grow up.

