That seems unnecessarily extreme. If you have any influence on policy,
with one line you can bring 100 years of library intellectual freedom
experience to your computer system. Just add a line like this one
(from the University of Wisconsin at Milwaulkee's Netnews policy) to
your computer policy:
       
     [T]he same standards and principles of intellectual and academic
     freedom developed for university libraries be applied to material
     received from the news network.

If you don't have influence on policy, get some. Lobby the University
Senate, the Chancellor, or the appropriate committees. Or, if you have
wider interest, run for the Senate or volunteer for committees.

At U. of Illinois, I did most of my lobbying electronically. I used
email, the uiuc.general newsgroup, lists of committees and members
from the staff newspaper, and the on-line university directory. Not
everything was on-line at first, but when asked nicely both the staff
newspaper and the Senate office were willing to give me copies of word
processor files on diskette and permission to convert them to plain
ascii and distribute them.

Other schools have had good luck with electronic petitions. At Stanford,
a petition caused a new committee to be created, that committee got
library-principles applied to newsgroups. Other school have had
student groups form.

You are not powerless. Students and faculty have a voice in setting
university policy.

